  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 254 AM EDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nPattern Synopsis: \n\nLow-amplitude midlevel ridging continues to remain settled over the \nnorthwoods with closed midlevel low pressure over the \nMississippi/Ohio Valley today tied to the remnants of Isaias. This \nridging will support enough subsidence aloft to keep conditions dry \nfor our CWA through Monday while low-level warm air advection keeps \nour temperatures well above normal for early October. At the same \ntime, a disheveled wave develops surface low pressure lee of the \nCanadian Rockies and pushes moisture toward the Great Lakes Region \nlate Monday night. Subsequent rainfall will slowly progress across \nNorthern Michigan through Tuesday with most areas likely observing \nQPF amounts of a quarter inch or less. \n\nSurface high pressure builds on the backside of Tuesday's frontal \nboundary with a northerly cP air mass influence. Temperatures return \nto normal for the remainder of the forecast period with highs in the \n50s for most areas accompanied by surface high pressure centered \nover Ontario/Manitoba. Only chance of rainfall beyond the midweek \nremains around the Friday timeframe as long range ensembles depict a \nweak clipper system digging across the Great Lakes Region, but \nimpacts associated with rainfall amounts remain low at this time.\n\nForecast Details: \n\nToday and Monday: Beautiful fall conditions with summer-like \ntemperatures continue today as aforementioned subsidence with \ntroughing to our south supports dry weather with highs in the 70s \nfor most areas. Some alto and cirrostratus cloud cover will build \nacross the northern lower as the remnants of Isaias progress across \nthe Southern Great Lakes Region. Same conditions continue for Monday \nwith temperatures remaining slightly cooler due to the approaching \nweak frontal boundary to our northwest. Nevertheless, widespread \nhighs in the 60s and 70s will persist before fall like temperatures \nreturn through the remainder of the forecast period. \n\nTuesday through Saturday Outlook: Well advertised rainfall will \ncommence Monday night across eastern upper before slowly making its \nway across northern lower. Latest model soundings depict deeper and \nwarmer saturated profiles attached to subtropical moisture over the \nAmerican Southwest. Latest guidance keeps QPF totals around a \nquarter inch or less for most areas, but some localized areas could \nobserve higher amounts in excess of a half inch as the center of low \npressure crosses the Northern Lower. The rest of the week remains \nrather quiet as high pressure builds to our north accompanied by \nhighs turning back to normal (50s). Low precipitation chances remain \nin the forecast as long-range ensembles depict a clipper developing \nover Alberta and digging across the Midwest around the Friday \ntimeframe. At this time, guidance keeps most rainfall during the \nday, so the threat of any different p-type moving into the forecast \nremains low as we head into mid October.",
